WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.054
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.054 --> 00:00:10.271
Ray, we are so excited to have
you here tonight at Stanford.

00:00:10.271 --> 00:00:11.281
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:11.281 --> 00:00:13.381
&gt;&gt; I'm so excited to be here.

00:00:13.381 --> 00:00:16.534
&gt;&gt; Well, Ray,
as our little tribute to Bridgewater,

00:00:16.534 --> 00:00:19.340
we are going to videotape
this conversation.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:20.421
&gt;&gt; Okay, [LAUGH].

00:00:20.421 --> 00:00:23.229
&gt;&gt; But
I had to veto any of the live scoring, so

00:00:23.229 --> 00:00:26.751
I'm just not at your level
on handling feedback yet.

00:00:26.751 --> 00:00:30.243
We've got a lot to cover,
your life, your principles, and

00:00:30.243 --> 00:00:32.102
how you think about the future.

00:00:32.102 --> 00:00:36.329
But before we start, I want to get
a feel for our audience here tonight.

00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:40.327
So, please raise your
hand if you have read or

00:00:40.327 --> 00:00:43.924
are reading Ray's book, Principles.

00:00:43.924 --> 00:00:45.546
Wow.
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:00:45.546 --> 00:00:46.757
&gt;&gt; Pretty good.

00:00:46.757 --> 00:00:51.075
Okay, and so the rest of you, how many
of you are planning to read Principles?

00:00:51.075 --> 00:00:54.479
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, they're an ambitious group.

00:00:54.479 --> 00:00:57.895
For anyone else out there, do you at
least have your own moral principles?

00:00:57.895 --> 00:01:00.435
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, okay, good, good.

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:05.142
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Let's start back in the early days on

00:01:05.142 --> 00:01:06.141
Long Island.

00:01:06.141 --> 00:01:09.122
Now, you were quite
the capitalist growing up.

00:01:09.122 --> 00:01:14.881
You were a paper boy, a lawn mower,
a snow shoveler, and a golf caddy.

00:01:14.881 --> 00:01:17.089
And at the ripe old age of age of 12,

00:01:17.089 --> 00:01:20.231
you made your first investment
in the stock market.

00:01:20.231 --> 00:01:24.021
Talk to us about how that even happens.

00:01:24.021 --> 00:01:29.141
&gt;&gt; Well, everybody was talking about the
stock market, it was no big deal really.

00:01:29.141 --> 00:01:32.188
[COUGH] At the time in the 60s,

00:01:32.188 --> 00:01:37.121
the stock market was
the hardest it has ever been.

00:01:37.121 --> 00:01:38.541
Literally if you got a haircut or

00:01:38.541 --> 00:01:41.391
anything, everybody would talk
to you about the stock market.

00:01:41.391 --> 00:01:46.297
And so when I was cutting, they were
talking to me about the stock market,

00:01:46.297 --> 00:01:49.872
And I had to take my $6 a bag,
I would take my $12.

00:01:49.872 --> 00:01:53.132
And when I got $50,
I had put it in the stock market.

00:01:53.132 --> 00:01:54.630
It was just a natural thing to do.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.031
&gt;&gt; And
it did pretty well the first investment?

00:01:57.031 --> 00:02:01.826
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it was the only company that
I ever heard of that was selling for

00:02:01.826 --> 00:02:03.491
less than $5 a share.

00:02:03.491 --> 00:02:07.590
So I figured I could buy more shares which
meant if it went up I'd make more money.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.720
That was my rationale,
that was a stupid rationale, right?

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.690
And it was a company that
was about to go broke.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.921
But another company acquired it,
it tripled in value and

00:02:17.921 --> 00:02:20.961
I thought this game is easy,
I can make a lot of money.

00:02:20.961 --> 00:02:24.032
Games is not easy but
that's what got me hooked.

00:02:24.032 --> 00:02:26.337
&gt;&gt; So
you got a bit of a rolling start, but

00:02:26.337 --> 00:02:30.030
you continue to invest through
high school and university.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:32.386
And you later enrolled
at a business school,

00:02:32.386 --> 00:02:34.686
one we like to call
the Stanford of the East.

00:02:34.686 --> 00:02:38.961
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Were you met, of course, Joel Peterson.

00:02:38.961 --> 00:02:43.471
&gt;&gt; Joel was in my head group and
we have good memories.

00:02:43.471 --> 00:02:45.934
&gt;&gt; Now we'll talk about those later.

00:02:45.934 --> 00:02:48.996
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now, Ray, for your summer internship,

00:02:48.996 --> 00:02:51.863
many of your classmates were
going to Wall Street and

00:02:51.863 --> 00:02:55.332
they were seeking these glamorous
equity trading positions.

00:02:55.332 --> 00:02:59.400
You on the other hand sought out
a role in commodities trading,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:04.236
which at the time wasn't just less
prestigious, it was pretty obscure for

00:03:04.236 --> 00:03:06.640
an MBA to seek out that kind of a role.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.701
So talk to us about what that experience
of going against the grain taught you.

00:03:12.701 --> 00:03:18.231
&gt;&gt; Well, I figured it had low
margin requirements, right?

00:03:18.231 --> 00:03:21.650
You only had to basically put
up about 10% of the money.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:24.702
So I figured if you're going to be right,
you'll make more money,

00:03:24.702 --> 00:03:28.041
because of low margin requirements,
so I got hooked on commodity trading.

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:31.180
And then there wasn't anybody who
would go from Harvard Business School.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:35.860
So this was 1972, the summer of 1972,

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:40.189
and I went to the director
of commodities and

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:44.060
I begged for a job, and he gave me a job.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:50.313
And that was just before the 1973
oil crisis and the oil shock, and

00:03:50.313 --> 00:03:55.521
then Wall Street went to hall and
commodities became hot.

00:03:55.521 --> 00:03:58.640
And I was lucky, and so
I went into commodities.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.373
&gt;&gt; So you were a hot property by second

00:04:01.373 --> 00:04:04.220
year and-
&gt;&gt; I never really knew anything about

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:08.272
commodities other than the summer job,
but I was then a hot property,

00:04:08.272 --> 00:04:12.342
because there was no Harvard Business
School type person in commodities.

00:04:12.342 --> 00:04:13.872
&gt;&gt; I see.
&gt;&gt; So I got a job,

00:04:13.872 --> 00:04:17.508
which was stupid to hire me, but they did.

00:04:17.508 --> 00:04:22.049
And then that company
then got into trouble.

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:26.134
Then I went to another company and
I did that for about a year, and

00:04:26.134 --> 00:04:28.037
then started individually.

00:04:28.037 --> 00:04:30.910
&gt;&gt; I just want to,
you skipped over something there I know.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.034
Many of us here in this room,

00:04:33.034 --> 00:04:37.470
we know you as a master of
thoughtful disagreement, okay?

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.549
But you didn't have that concept fully
nailed back in those early days.

00:04:41.549 --> 00:04:48.689
So talk to us about that disagreement
you had with your boss on New Years Eve.

00:04:48.689 --> 00:04:54.143
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean we got drunk and

00:04:54.143 --> 00:05:00.698
then I slugged him,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:00.698 --> 00:05:04.059
&gt;&gt; And he was a good guy.

00:05:04.059 --> 00:05:10.579
What he did, but he drove home,
he totaled his car.

00:05:10.579 --> 00:05:14.399
His wife chewed him out because
he had missed her party,

00:05:14.399 --> 00:05:18.469
and he came in with a black eye,
and he didn't fire me then.

00:05:18.469 --> 00:05:19.189
&gt;&gt; He didn't fire you.

00:05:19.189 --> 00:05:20.819
&gt;&gt; He did not fire me then.

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:22.345
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; And then it was another

00:05:22.345 --> 00:05:24.760
incident that caused him to fire me.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:26.584
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:05:26.584 --> 00:05:31.693
I want to know what got you fired,
can you tell us?

00:05:31.693 --> 00:05:33.465
&gt;&gt; Anyway the.

00:05:33.465 --> 00:05:37.104
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Anyway, that was the best thing that

00:05:37.104 --> 00:05:40.849
actually happened to me
because then I was on my own.

00:05:40.849 --> 00:05:44.090
&gt;&gt; Some of our career advisors around
here we might frame that one as

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:45.119
passionate right?

00:05:45.119 --> 00:05:49.054
And,
&gt;&gt; It was it's kind of in retrospect,

00:05:49.054 --> 00:05:51.622
it was a stupid thing to do, right?

00:05:51.622 --> 00:05:58.071
But it was just what it was
like at the time I guess.

00:05:58.071 --> 00:05:59.483
&gt;&gt; And so you go ahead and

00:05:59.483 --> 00:06:04.520
start Bridgewater Associates from your
two bedroom apartment in New York.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:08.894
So you're 26 years old,
you're a first time entrepreneur and

00:06:08.894 --> 00:06:10.729
a professional investor.

00:06:10.729 --> 00:06:15.709
Now, some things those roles would seem
to have in common is you have a high risk

00:06:15.709 --> 00:06:18.061
of being wrong and making mistakes.

00:06:18.061 --> 00:06:21.820
Talk to us about your
relationship with mistakes, and

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:24.280
how those lead to your principles.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:26.051
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think,

00:06:32.174 --> 00:06:36.390
Just generally as an entrepreneur or
in the markets,

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:40.898
you have to think differently
in order to be successful.

00:06:40.898 --> 00:06:44.603
In the markets whatever the view is
is built into the price because it

00:06:44.603 --> 00:06:48.633
reflects the consensus And as an
entrepreneur starting out a business you

00:06:48.633 --> 00:06:52.409
have to think differently, and
there's a high risk of being wrong.

00:06:52.409 --> 00:06:56.822
And so what I learned over that period,
you have to be audacious,

00:06:56.822 --> 00:07:00.995
you have to think differently,
you have to be confident, and

00:07:00.995 --> 00:07:04.549
then you also experience
a high risk of being wrong.

00:07:04.549 --> 00:07:09.154
And I think we're going to go to a clip
of one of the really big mistakes

00:07:09.154 --> 00:07:13.527
that I made that changed my whole
approach to decision making.

00:07:13.527 --> 00:07:18.638
So, I in 1980, 81,
I had calculated that American banks

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:24.035
had lent a lot more money to foreign
countries, and those countries

00:07:24.035 --> 00:07:29.829
were going to be able to pay back, and
that we were going to have a debt crisis.

00:07:29.829 --> 00:07:33.120
And I thought that that would
cause an economic collapse.

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:37.997
And in August 1982,
Mexico defaulted on its debt,

00:07:37.997 --> 00:07:41.749
and I received a lot of attention for
that.

00:07:41.749 --> 00:07:46.282
And I was asked to speak to
Congress at that time, and

00:07:46.282 --> 00:07:48.969
I was also on Wall Street Week.

00:07:48.969 --> 00:07:54.019
And we brought a clip of that,
so you'll get a sense of that.

00:07:54.019 --> 00:07:57.248
So Zach, can you hit the button and
show the clip?

00:07:57.248 --> 00:08:02.040
Mr. Chairman, Mr. Mitchell, it's a great
pleasure and a great honor to be able to

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:07.360
appear before you in examination with
what is going wrong with our economy.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.520
The economy is now flat,
teetering on the brink of failure.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.450
&gt;&gt; You were recently quoted in an article.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:18.100
You said, I can say this with absolute
certainty because I know how markets work.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:21.760
&gt;&gt; I can say with absolute certainty that
if you look at the liquidity base in

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:26.730
the corporations and the world as a whole,
that there's such a reduced level of

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:32.010
liquidity that you can't return
to an era of static flation.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.890
&gt;&gt; Wasn't I arrogant?

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:35.710
Incredibly arrogant.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:38.390
I couldn't have been more wrong.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.540
I was so wrong.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.699
That was the exact bottom in
the stock market, August 1982.

00:08:44.699 --> 00:08:47.820
Mexico defaulted on its debt,
it was the bottom in the stock market.

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:49.490
We had a big bull market.

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:54.550
And I had then I think like eight people
at Bridgewater, and I had to let them go.

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:58.560
And I was so broke

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:04.440
that I had to borrow $4,000 from my dad
in order to pay for my family bills.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.398
And that was very painful.

00:09:07.398 --> 00:09:12.010
And it changed my whole approach
to decision making, because

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:17.460
it made me think in the future,
how do I know I'm not wrong?

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.890
How do I know I'm right?

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:24.200
It gave me the humility that I needed
to balance with my audacity, and

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:29.330
it made me bring in people
that would disagree with me.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:33.240
So I wanted to find the smartest people
that would disagree with me, and

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:39.390
I really started to think about how
would I deal with disagreement.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:43.571
And I would say that this is a big thing.

00:09:43.571 --> 00:09:45.780
It's such a pleasure to be here,
by the way,

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:49.622
because you're beginning this phase
of your career, you're just about to,

00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:52.200
this what I would call
the second phase of your life.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.550
First phase of your life is when you're
dependent on others and you're learning.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:00.660
Second phase of your life
is when you're working.

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:03.670
And then eventually others
become dependent on you.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:06.390
And in that second phase,
it's totally different.

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:09.040
And I'm going into my third phase.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.880
And so to be able to sort of
pass along some things that

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:15.620
were helpful to me is a treat.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:17.270
And along those lines,

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:22.630
I would say you're going to keep
having failures as well as successes.

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.810
And it's in the failures
that you learn the most.

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:31.050
And so my attitude began to change
in terms of making decisions.

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:36.080
Most importantly, I would view every

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:42.140
failure as a puzzle that would give
me a gem if I could solve the puzzle.

00:10:42.140 --> 00:10:46.591
And the puzzle was what would I
do differently in the future so

00:10:46.591 --> 00:10:49.568
that I wouldn't have that bad outcome?

00:10:49.568 --> 00:10:53.940
And if I could solve that, that would give
me the gem, and the gem was a principle.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:11:00.473
And the other thing that I did was write
down all my decision making principles.

00:11:00.473 --> 00:11:03.915
When I would make a decision,
I would take the time and

00:11:03.915 --> 00:11:08.270
say, what are my criteria for
making that decision?

00:11:08.270 --> 00:11:12.170
And that was so invaluable,
I'd want to pass that along to you.

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:15.866
You're going to encounter so
many things in your life, and

00:11:15.866 --> 00:11:19.031
you're just going to
make decisions like that.

00:11:19.031 --> 00:11:25.020
If instead you pause and you think, what
are my criteria for making that decision?

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:28.130
You write it down and
then you reflect on it.

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:31.900
You'll start to realize that the same
thing happens over and over again.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:35.690
And when the next one comes along,
you'll reflect on that principle and

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:36.900
you'll refine it.

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:40.650
And it serves the purpose also of
letting you communicate very effectively

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:41.210
with others.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:45.988
So when I started my business,
it was great because we would

00:11:45.988 --> 00:11:50.875
all know what we could expect and
why and that whole process.

00:11:50.875 --> 00:11:55.314
I mean I think it's really that
whole notion of the best comes from

00:11:55.314 --> 00:11:59.650
learning from mistakes and
doing that in a systematic way.

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:02.250
Failure is just part of the journey.

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:06.510
&gt;&gt; And to get those mistakes, Ray,
and to bring them out into the open,

00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:11.330
you needed an idea of meritocracy, and
for that you need a radical truth and

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:12.480
a radical transparency.

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:14.410
Talk to us a little bit
about those two concepts.

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:18.258
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I don't know, Zack,
do you have that diagram?

00:12:18.258 --> 00:12:19.290
Okay-
&gt;&gt; Zack's busy today.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.866
&gt;&gt; So this is-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:12:20.866 --> 00:12:23.900
&gt;&gt; This is the way it worked for me.

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:29.070
So in order to be successful,
I needed independent

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:33.800
thinkers who would disagree with me, and
that we would disagree with each other.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.840
Because otherwise,
it's that false confidence.

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:38.810
How do you stress test each
other to get to the best idea?

00:12:38.810 --> 00:12:42.100
So I needed independent thinkers
who are also wanting to go for

00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:43.960
the audacious goals.

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:45.980
So if you have independent thinkers,

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:49.120
how are you going to get
past the disagreement?

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:52.890
You actually have to have a system for
disagreeing well and

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:54.750
getting past the disagreement.

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:56.950
So I needed an idea meritocracy,

00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:02.490
in other words an actual process that
organizes disagreement to get past it.

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:06.700
And in order to do that,
then I needed to have that principle,

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:08.080
I needed to write it down, and

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:12.940
we all needed to know what that rules are,
and then we need to make these systems.

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:16.760
And then when we did that,
that changed everything, right,

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:18.350
that thoughtful disagreement.

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:21.350
Because it's collective
decision making in which you

00:13:21.350 --> 00:13:26.060
significantly increase the probabilities
of making a right decision.

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:29.110
I think the greatest tragedy of people,

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:34.260
probably one of the greatest tragedies
of mankind, is people who hold opinions

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:39.390
in their mind that they're attached
to that are wrong that they could so

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:43.480
easily stress test and significantly
raise the probability of being right.

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:45.040
So we needed to do that.

00:13:45.040 --> 00:13:48.660
And when we did that we added
our successes and our failures.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:54.050
But the people believed in that idea,
in meritocracy, and it was fair.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:59.344
Because the system, if you have a system
in which you can fight for your

00:13:59.344 --> 00:14:05.117
arguments and you know that the system's
going to be fair, you can care more.

00:14:05.117 --> 00:14:10.000
And so that's the type of organization
I needed, and that was the key, right.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:14.560
Whatever success I've had in my
life has had more to do with my

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:19.200
knowing how to deal with what I don't
know than because of anything I know.

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:22.817
And that idea meritocracy,
that triangulation with smart people.

00:14:22.817 --> 00:14:26.550
&gt;&gt; So Ray, I want to go a layer
deeper on this radical truth and

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:27.540
radical transparency.

00:14:27.540 --> 00:14:29.524
I think for many of us hearing it here,

00:14:29.524 --> 00:14:32.816
it's very hard to argue with
the logic of what you're saying.

00:14:32.816 --> 00:14:37.726
And yet the culture at Bridgewater feels
quite foreign to what most of us have

00:14:37.726 --> 00:14:41.860
experienced in our personal or
professional lives.

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:46.830
What is it about this concept that is so
difficult for us to embrace?

00:14:46.830 --> 00:14:51.297
&gt;&gt; By the way,
that's the competitive edge, right,

00:14:51.297 --> 00:14:56.690
because if you were to say what's
the edge, that's the edge.

00:14:56.690 --> 00:15:02.940
I think we have ego and blind spots
barriers that stand in our way.

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:08.090
And so that notion of attach to what
you know is such a big thing for

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:09.600
your ego or your blind spot.

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:17.520
And so there's this subliminal
reaction when somebody disagrees.

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:20.060
Rather than producing curiosity,

00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:24.530
it produces antagonism,
it's an amygdala reaction.

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:28.288
And I think a lot has to do
with the education system too.

00:15:28.288 --> 00:15:32.340
You're rewarded for knowing or
you were rewarded for not making mistakes,

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:35.400
rather than knowing what
your weaknesses are.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:37.240
So in order to go through that,

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:42.530
one has to intellectually go through
it to say it doesn't makes sense.

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:44.984
So let's say we're going to
have a relationship].

00:15:44.984 --> 00:15:47.924
And you're now, you're working there,
you're my partner.

00:15:47.924 --> 00:15:48.424
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:15:48.424 --> 00:15:50.484
&gt;&gt; And I [LAUGH] Yeah?

00:15:50.484 --> 00:15:53.604
What till you see what it's like.

00:15:53.604 --> 00:15:58.860
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] But you would say, I would say

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:03.811
Do you want me to know,
do you want to know what I really think?

00:16:03.811 --> 00:16:06.869
Do you want to tell me
what you really think?

00:16:06.869 --> 00:16:09.168
Do you want to know your weaknesses?

00:16:09.168 --> 00:16:10.787
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:16:10.787 --> 00:16:12.804
&gt;&gt; Do you want me to know my weaknesses?

00:16:12.804 --> 00:16:15.376
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Intellectually,

00:16:15.376 --> 00:16:18.830
you would say you would
want to know your weaknesses.

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:21.670
Why wouldn't you want to
know your weaknesses?

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:27.520
Emotionally, that can be difficult so
it takes adapting to.

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:31.360
So we describe it as there's two yous.

00:16:31.360 --> 00:16:38.690
There's the, and this is correct in terms
of literally, there is a conscious,

00:16:38.690 --> 00:16:42.380
thoughtful you that might say,
I would like me to do those things.

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:45.930
I'd like to know my weaknesses so
I can know how to deal with them.

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:49.220
And then there is an emotional
you that's subliminal.

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:53.750
And it's really the battle with your
two yous that you have with yourself.

00:16:53.750 --> 00:16:57.810
And once people start to realize that and
they come in that environment, then they

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:01.640
see that they're really more struggling
with themselves than each other.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:08.080
So, that radical truthfulness, so
the right way it is is, in order to

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:12.940
have the radical truthfulness, you have to
put what you honestly think on the table.

00:17:12.940 --> 00:17:14.550
Can you do that?

00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:17.910
And then, transparency means that
you can get to see everything.

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:23.020
So, what I did was put,
everybody could see everything,

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:24.680
so that was the main thing.

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:29.420
So when I would make decisions I would,
I’d be doing, I'd be very, very radically

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:34.230
open about what I was doing and the most
difficult decisions that we would make.

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:38.100
And then I would refer
to the principles and

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:41.960
then I’d have everybody challenge
me on whether that made sense.

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:47.850
And so our intellectual approach
overcame our emotional barriers.

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:50.544
But I think it's like that for physical.

00:17:50.544 --> 00:17:55.010
You know, I was, H.R. McMaster's here and

00:17:55.010 --> 00:18:00.180
he was a great host to me down and
I got to see Ranger School.

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:03.618
And I think about those people
pushing themselves, and

00:18:03.618 --> 00:18:05.820
how they were pushing themselves.

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:09.171
And they were doing the physical
equivalent, although the dental was

00:18:09.171 --> 00:18:13.700
equally important, and that produces that
particular stream, they wanted that.

00:18:13.700 --> 00:18:15.750
And that's what is about.

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:16.810
But it's not for everybody,

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:22.780
just like some people would
watch out of the Army Rangers.

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:25.990
It's not for everybody, but it's powerful.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:30.570
Because you build meaningful work and
meaningful relationships.

00:18:30.570 --> 00:18:34.480
In other words,
I think that that's the power.

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:37.610
If you are committed to your mission,
like you're excited,

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:42.370
we're going to do this thing together,
maybe a start up or maybe,

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:45.600
whatever it is,
you're on that mission together.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.690
And then at the same time
you're on that mission,

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:51.630
you have these meaningful relationships,
which means total honesty.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:55.860
And with that total honesty and that
meaningful relationship, you get a joy

00:18:55.860 --> 00:19:00.080
out of the relationship as well as
you get a joy about being successful.

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:02.510
And it doesn't have that two-faced nature.

00:19:02.510 --> 00:19:04.960
It doesn't have politics behind it.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:08.390
It's just straightforward, so
yeah, that's what it's like.

00:19:09.730 --> 00:19:11.140
&gt;&gt; So that's really what you're avoiding.

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:12.300
It's the kind of politics and

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:14.810
the gossiping that you might
get in the normal world.

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:18.190
Albeit it's not for everyone,
not everyone can kind of take this.

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:24.100
&gt;&gt; It changes the ethic like there is
no talking behind somebody's back.

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:28.898
And the reason it is, first of all
it's unproductive, if you're talking

00:19:28.898 --> 00:19:33.944
behind somebody's back then you're not
dealing with the particular issue.

00:19:33.944 --> 00:19:38.901
And in a place like where we value anybody
speaking up with their point of view,

00:19:38.901 --> 00:19:42.600
then you can raise it, and
you can then deal with it.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:47.282
But also,
it then becomes sort of an ethic thing.

00:19:47.282 --> 00:19:52.280
Like don't you have the guts to
talk about what you're saying?

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:54.350
And so if you have the guts, so

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:58.140
it's almost humiliating to
talk behind somebody's back.

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:02.050
And so
that standard then carries forward and

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:06.560
it leads to that kind of radical truth
[INAUDIBLE] radical transparency.

00:20:06.560 --> 00:20:09.810
So you have some people
who it's not at all for.

00:20:09.810 --> 00:20:13.260
And then you have people who couldn't
work anywhere else because they

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:16.675
wouldn't want to work, they go in
the company and everybody's smiling and

00:20:16.675 --> 00:20:17.980
they're giving high fives and whatever.

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:19.630
And they're all talking
behind each others' backs,

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:22.870
there's a lot of politics going on,
and that they can't deal with.

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:26.640
So a lot of people wouldn't
want to work anywhere else, and

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:28.570
some people it's not the right place for.

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:32.700
&gt;&gt; So Ray, you've built a machine,
really, that supports this culture.

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:37.512
And you also have a belief
that humans are machines.

00:20:37.512 --> 00:20:42.269
I think few companies have done more
than Bridgewater to try and use data to

00:20:42.269 --> 00:20:48.720
understand their employees, to put their
attributes and their talents into numbers.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:51.780
So I'm curious if you
see attention between

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:55.240
this explicit labeling
of a person's abilities

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:59.370
with what Carol Dwight at Stanford
might call this growth mindset.

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:02.550
The idea that a person's abilities,
talents,

00:21:02.550 --> 00:21:06.490
even intelligence is more fluid and
can change.

00:21:06.490 --> 00:21:10.890
&gt;&gt; Well I think, I'm just trying to be
as accurate as possible and change,

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:15.850
if a change occurs, then it's reflected in
the numbers and everybody sees the change.

00:21:15.850 --> 00:21:17.800
&gt;&gt; So it's a lot of.

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:23.340
&gt;&gt; But I think that at any point in time,
you have to be realistic of where you are.

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:28.090
And you have to recognize that people
have different strengths and weaknesses.

00:21:28.090 --> 00:21:31.425
And if you're not dealing with that,
that's a particular problem,

00:21:31.425 --> 00:21:33.885
stands in the way of the growth
of the particular people.

00:21:33.885 --> 00:21:37.735
And then, also people making choices
about other people there, right?

00:21:37.735 --> 00:21:40.945
So if I'm your boss over
making that decision,

00:21:40.945 --> 00:21:45.265
if I'm not open, if I don't give you
that constant feedback, and also

00:21:45.265 --> 00:21:49.570
if I'm not collecting data from others
because I'm just not sure that I'm right.

00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:52.190
So the data kind of helps to speak for

00:21:52.190 --> 00:21:56.500
itself in terms of understanding
what a person is like.

00:21:56.500 --> 00:22:00.878
So I think, you know, it's a good thing.

00:22:00.878 --> 00:22:04.520
Though it doesn't mean that you
stand in the way of evolution.

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:08.860
It means that to whatever extent
evolution occurs, you get to see it.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:12.700
&gt;&gt; So I think the bigger context here
raised, many of us will be graduating

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:16.300
in a few months and
some of us will be making a choice.

00:22:16.300 --> 00:22:21.269
Do we return to a job and a function
we know how to do that suits us well or

00:22:21.269 --> 00:22:24.469
do we branch out down a new,
untested path?

00:22:24.469 --> 00:22:26.670
From what you've seen at Bridgewater,

00:22:26.670 --> 00:22:29.480
what might you advise us
in how we think about that?

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:30.520
&gt;&gt; This is the time for

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:34.170
your adventure, you;re experimenting,
you're learning, right?

00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:37.410
For that experimentation.

00:22:37.410 --> 00:22:42.402
And I'll guarantee you that wherever
you're going to be in five years is

00:22:42.402 --> 00:22:43.680
going to change.

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:47.110
Go for the experience.

00:22:47.110 --> 00:22:51.160
But I think the most important thing
I would say when you're going on

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:55.640
that adventure is to understand
how to triangulate well.

00:22:56.660 --> 00:23:01.415
What I mean is find the three or
four smartest people who are dealing with

00:23:01.415 --> 00:23:06.014
the most important questions that
you're facing, particularly if

00:23:06.014 --> 00:23:11.100
they're willing to disagree with each
other and disagree with you on that.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:16.225
And then with that triangulation, where
there are areas of disagreement that'll

00:23:16.225 --> 00:23:21.070
bubble to the surface and you'll find
out what might be, you'll learn a lot.

00:23:21.070 --> 00:23:23.472
And then through that humility,

00:23:23.472 --> 00:23:28.920
you go through experiences, and
make the most out of your failures.

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:32.800
Be adventurous,
make the discovery, go for it.

00:23:32.800 --> 00:23:33.310
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:34.130
&gt;&gt; Right?

00:23:34.130 --> 00:23:35.998
&gt;&gt; All right, well, we're done,
thank you very much.

00:23:35.998 --> 00:23:39.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Joking, but the other question we face,

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:42.050
Ray, is what type of
organization do we join?

00:23:42.050 --> 00:23:43.390
So-
&gt;&gt; I think culture's the most

00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:44.200
important thing.

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:47.520
I think who you're with-
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:23:47.520 --> 00:23:51.670
&gt;&gt; And where you are is much
more important than what you do.

00:23:53.080 --> 00:23:55.200
&gt;&gt; Sorry, let me take you up on that.

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:57.160
So let's assume you're in our shoes.

00:23:57.160 --> 00:24:00.670
You're about to go into the working world,
and you want to work.

00:24:00.670 --> 00:24:02.930
In an idea meritocracy.

00:24:02.930 --> 00:24:06.110
Many in this room are going
to join large organizations

00:24:06.110 --> 00:24:09.920
that have embedded hierarchies,
cultures, and norms.

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:14.232
So talk to us in a practical sense about
how we can bring some of these ideas to

00:24:14.232 --> 00:24:15.201
the workplace.

00:24:15.201 --> 00:24:18.630
&gt;&gt; Well, you have the choice to get
work at wherever culture you want.

00:24:18.630 --> 00:24:21.610
The most important thing is,
do you work in the culture?

00:24:21.610 --> 00:24:24.380
Now you were raised here
in your education and

00:24:24.380 --> 00:24:29.480
before to try to have the opportunity
to make sense of things.

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:30.180
Okay.

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:34.790
So I would go into your job and say,
if something doesn't make sense to me,

00:24:34.790 --> 00:24:38.350
do I have the right to say it
doesn't make sense to me and

00:24:38.350 --> 00:24:43.150
ask you why it makes sense and
to be able to probe you?

00:24:43.150 --> 00:24:44.630
Or do I not have that right?

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:49.811
And I think you should think hard about
whether you want to be in the organization

00:24:49.811 --> 00:24:51.650
that doesn't give you that right.

00:24:51.650 --> 00:24:56.367
So I think the most fundamental
thing is to be in that

00:24:56.367 --> 00:24:59.889
kind of organization with humility.

00:24:59.889 --> 00:25:04.139
In other words recognizing that you might
not but you always have the right to make

00:25:04.139 --> 00:25:08.780
sense of things, and if something doesn't
make sense, to say it doesn't make sense.

00:25:08.780 --> 00:25:13.050
So I would say that that would be the most
important thing for me if you're going to

00:25:13.050 --> 00:25:18.210
learn, because otherwise you're going to
be somebody's schlep, they're going to say

00:25:18.210 --> 00:25:23.450
do these things and you're just going to
run and follow their instructions and

00:25:23.450 --> 00:25:26.730
so on, and you're not going to learn
if you're following their instructions.

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:28.510
because you have to think for yourself.

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:29.750
I think.

00:25:29.750 --> 00:25:32.440
&gt;&gt; And you've given us plenty
of tools to do that, right?

00:25:32.440 --> 00:25:34.520
I want to talk a little
bit about your legacy.

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:37.270
You've accummulated these
principles over 40 years.

00:25:37.270 --> 00:25:40.890
You've decided to share them as
part of what you're calling this

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:42.700
third phase in your life.

00:25:42.700 --> 00:25:48.100
A question Joel often asks the class is,
what does winning look like to you?

00:25:48.100 --> 00:25:53.620
You've invested huge amounts of your time
and energy in this public facing role.

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:55.870
What does winning look
like in this third phase?

00:25:55.870 --> 00:25:59.854
&gt;&gt; I think that through life it's all
just a matter of personal evolution and

00:25:59.854 --> 00:26:01.470
contributing to evolution.

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:06.654
For me, you evolve and
there's an instinctual reaction

00:26:06.654 --> 00:26:11.838
that I had that what I wanted
to do was pass along the things

00:26:11.838 --> 00:26:16.276
that are valuable to me so
I can move beyond that.

00:26:16.276 --> 00:26:21.872
And, My son at 2014 when I
was making this transition

00:26:21.872 --> 00:26:26.690
gave me this book,
Joseph's Campbell's Hero of 1,000 Faces.

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:29.580
And he describes these
various phases of our lives.

00:26:29.580 --> 00:26:33.550
Which I related to and
I won't take you through them all.

00:26:33.550 --> 00:26:35.380
But there's the call to adventure.

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:36.190
You go on an adventure.

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:37.210
You have your ups and downs.

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:38.850
You reflect on them and so on.

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:41.050
And then when you get to
this stage in your life,

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:46.220
the thing that you want more
is not to be successful.

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:46.960
You've done it.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:48.680
You don't want to be more successful.

00:26:48.680 --> 00:26:52.050
You want to help others be
successful without you.

00:26:52.050 --> 00:26:54.200
And that's what I feel.

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:57.300
And then when I feel like
if I've done that best job,

00:26:57.300 --> 00:26:59.630
I will have done my best job,
and we're good.

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:03.080
And so that's my instinctual
reaction at this time.

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:05.540
&gt;&gt; Well you've given us a lot to start and

00:27:05.540 --> 00:27:08.180
I know there's plenty out there
who haven't read the books so

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:09.780
you're not quite there yet, but.

00:27:09.780 --> 00:27:17.090
&gt;&gt; I want to say just for everybody and
I'll mention it maybe later.

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:22.930
What I did was, there's the book and
then I converted the book

00:27:22.930 --> 00:27:28.210
into an app that has the actual
cases of bridge waters.

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:30.344
So you could go into meetings and

00:27:30.344 --> 00:27:35.200
you can actually see this in action
we call it principles in action.

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:40.320
It'd a free app on the Apple store and

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:43.620
it'll take you in to that so that you
get a sense of what the thing is like.

00:27:43.620 --> 00:27:46.840
&gt;&gt; Plenty more case studies we love those,
Ray.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:47.813
So it's all good.

00:27:47.813 --> 00:27:48.776
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now, Ray,

00:27:48.776 --> 00:27:52.508
we could spend another hour on these
principles but unfortunately you've got

00:27:52.508 --> 00:27:56.476
far too many other perspectives we want to
probe you on, let's start with history.

00:27:56.476 --> 00:27:58.279
You're a student of history,

00:27:58.279 --> 00:28:02.657
you've stated that everything that
happens is just another one of those.

00:28:02.657 --> 00:28:07.198
So I'm curious when you look at today,
our stage in the economic cycle,

00:28:07.198 --> 00:28:11.230
our political tensions,
our geopolitical tensions.

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:14.020
What's the historical precedent for
what you see?

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:16.530
And what does that tell us
about what happens next?

00:28:16.530 --> 00:28:21.420
&gt;&gt; Well, I think there
are cause-effect relationships,

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:24.650
and then they repeat through history.

00:28:24.650 --> 00:28:29.080
I think it's important to not just assume
that because they happen in the past

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:32.440
that they'll happen again but also to
understand the cause effect relationship.

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:35.370
So in answering your question,
I'd like to refer to both.

00:28:35.370 --> 00:28:41.900
One of the things that I learned was that
I was, almost every time I was surprised,

00:28:41.900 --> 00:28:48.030
I was surprised by things that did
not happen in my life time but

00:28:48.030 --> 00:28:53.390
happened many times before and
as a result of that, I really

00:28:53.390 --> 00:28:58.190
found that I really needed to understand
history and why things happened before.

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:03.560
And that in 2007, because I studied
what happened in the Great Depression,

00:29:03.560 --> 00:29:08.430
we were able to anticipate
the 2008 financial crisis.

00:29:08.430 --> 00:29:12.410
Because of what happened in 1929 and
32, so

00:29:12.410 --> 00:29:19.140
the period that I think is most analogous
is the late 1930s and I'll explain why.

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:24.449
And this is just the mechanics of it.

00:29:24.449 --> 00:29:31.241
We had a debt crisis in 1932,
1929 to 1932.

00:29:31.241 --> 00:29:35.620
And in both of those cases,
interest rates hit zero.

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:38.530
Only two times in the century
that that happened.

00:29:38.530 --> 00:29:40.990
When interest rates hit zero,

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:45.490
the central banks had to print money and
buy financial assets.

00:29:45.490 --> 00:29:47.669
We call that quantitative easing.

00:29:47.669 --> 00:29:53.679
Those purchases of financial assets
puts financial asset prices up and

00:29:53.679 --> 00:29:58.596
as a result of pushing that up,
it makes the economy go up.

00:29:58.596 --> 00:30:03.910
So 1932 to 1937, the economy,
the stock market went up.

00:30:03.910 --> 00:30:11.270
Everything went up, in much the same
way as it did from 2009 till recently.

00:30:11.270 --> 00:30:16.700
And then, but because they
bought financial assets, those

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:22.360
with financial assets benefited more than
those who didn't have financial assets.

00:30:22.360 --> 00:30:28.000
And there were other factors like we have
technology today replacing people and

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:31.930
as a result, we have a large wealth gap.

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:36.380
So if you look today,
the top one tenth of one percent of

00:30:36.380 --> 00:30:41.710
the population's net worth is equal
to the bottom 90% combined and

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:45.920
if you take that on a measure like
that the wealth gap is the same

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:50.490
as it was in that period of time,
that 1930s period of time.

00:30:50.490 --> 00:30:54.200
And with a large wealth gap,
and differences in values,

00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:58.460
then you have populism,
the emergence of populism.

00:30:58.460 --> 00:31:01.100
Populism of the left and
populism of the right.

00:31:01.100 --> 00:31:05.990
And if you go through history, if you were
to say when did the last time you had

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:11.270
populism in major countries, it was in
that period of time for similar reasons.

00:31:11.270 --> 00:31:16.090
And with that time also you
had the classic rising of

00:31:16.090 --> 00:31:20.120
a power to challenge an existing power.

00:31:20.120 --> 00:31:24.600
In that case it was Germany
rising to challenge

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:29.930
the British Empire, and
then Japan and Asia.

00:31:29.930 --> 00:31:37.640
Like now we have China rising and
there's a rivalry, a conflict.

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:43.000
So the only periods in time that you
could see that was analogous recently,

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.080
the most recent period of time,
is like that.

00:31:45.080 --> 00:31:48.390
So I would say,
what are the big issues of the time?

00:31:48.390 --> 00:31:53.760
The big issues today are that there is

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:58.420
a limited ability of central
banks to ease monetary policy.

00:31:58.420 --> 00:32:02.740
because we have interest rates near
zero most cases around the world.

00:32:02.740 --> 00:32:06.700
And so the capacity of central
banks fees is limited.

00:32:06.700 --> 00:32:09.560
We have a very large wealth gap.

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:12.040
And with that, we have polarity,

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:17.920
a lot of political polarity, democracy
even being challenged by that polarity.

00:32:17.920 --> 00:32:20.860
And then you have these
conflicts around the world.

00:32:20.860 --> 00:32:22.800
And so that populism of the left and

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:26.260
populism of the right has
big economic implications.

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:29.530
What it'll mean for tax policy and

00:32:29.530 --> 00:32:34.020
what will it mean for
even conflicts between countries?

00:32:34.020 --> 00:32:36.940
And so
we have that rising power situation.

00:32:36.940 --> 00:32:40.740
So that period of time looks
most analagous to today.

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:43.480
&gt;&gt; You've covered a lot
of things there Ray and

00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:46.500
we'll try and dive into a few
with the time we have left.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:51.240
On a high level, it's a pretty
shocking comparison to make and

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:53.820
we know how the 1930s ended.

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:57.360
Do you believe that on
our current trajectory,

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:00.009
we're heading towards
some sort of conflict?

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:05.870
&gt;&gt; Well, we're certainly heading
to different kinds of conflict.

00:33:05.870 --> 00:33:08.840
The question I think is how
we're going to handle them.

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:14.480
We certainly are headed
domestically to lots of conflict.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:18.280
If you were to take the polls of,

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:24.760
the Republican Party is the most
conservative it has been ever.

00:33:24.760 --> 00:33:28.120
The Democratic Party is
the most liberal it is ever.

00:33:28.120 --> 00:33:36.375
The voting according to party lines
is the most it has been ever.

00:33:36.375 --> 00:33:39.760
94% of the votes are according
to party lines, and so

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:42.730
we certainly have an internal conflict.

00:33:42.730 --> 00:33:46.470
And that internal conflict has to
do about the combination, I think,

00:33:46.470 --> 00:33:51.470
of the economic conditions, the left and
the right, or let's say socialism and

00:33:51.470 --> 00:33:53.440
capitalism are issues.

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:57.570
And then there's also ways to be together.

00:33:57.570 --> 00:34:00.580
I would say I don't know

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:05.170
that we're clear on what principles
bind us together as a country.

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:07.580
I think we should go to principles.

00:34:07.580 --> 00:34:10.990
What principles bind us
together as a country?

00:34:10.990 --> 00:34:11.790
What do we believe in?

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:14.020
What are the principles that divide us?

00:34:14.020 --> 00:34:18.200
But anyway, we are having more
domestic conflict, I think.

00:34:18.200 --> 00:34:23.584
And then international conflict.

00:34:23.584 --> 00:34:26.250
There will be a challenge, a competition,

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:30.718
that is going on between the United States
and China and other places, but

00:34:30.718 --> 00:34:35.850
particularly the United States and
China which is a whole other discussion.

00:34:35.850 --> 00:34:42.340
That doesn't have to be a military
conflict though it could be.

00:34:42.340 --> 00:34:46.150
It depends I think on how
we handle the conflict.

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:51.360
So I think there's
the China-American relation that's

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:55.180
a topic week,
digress into it if you want to cover it.

00:34:55.180 --> 00:35:00.170
But I think it's all how are we
going to be with each other.

00:35:01.410 --> 00:35:06.758
So let say this issue
of the wealth gap and

00:35:06.758 --> 00:35:09.904
the left and the right,

00:35:09.904 --> 00:35:14.465
my big fear is that the conflict and

00:35:14.465 --> 00:35:18.419
then just badly engineered.

00:35:18.419 --> 00:35:24.200
Policies can cause us to have
worst conditions than we need to.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:27.440
There's enough resources to go around

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:31.100
to move us toward more equal
opportunities in various ways.

00:35:31.100 --> 00:35:32.690
We can deal with this together.

00:35:32.690 --> 00:35:37.440
But yeah, I think we're
an environment in which conflict is

00:35:37.440 --> 00:35:43.200
going to be more problematic than
we have actually experienced.

00:35:43.200 --> 00:35:44.590
&gt;&gt; Now, Ray, there's two issues.

00:35:44.590 --> 00:35:45.780
There's the external conflict.

00:35:45.780 --> 00:35:48.710
There's the internal
conflict in our own country.

00:35:48.710 --> 00:35:53.589
You've talked about redistribution, and
you've recently published a piece having

00:35:53.589 --> 00:35:57.881
why capitalism needs to be reformed and
a need for greater redistribution.

00:35:57.881 --> 00:36:04.336
Because it's tax day, April 15, would you
agree that a person of your resources and

00:36:04.336 --> 00:36:09.920
your wealth should be taxed more by
the US government to help further this?

00:36:11.250 --> 00:36:16.477
&gt;&gt; Yes, I believe that it's
inevitable that we should, yes.

00:36:16.477 --> 00:36:21.498
But I think the real question
is the most important is,

00:36:21.498 --> 00:36:27.960
are we striving to have a country,
what is our American dream?

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:31.860
Are we striving to have a country that is

00:36:31.860 --> 00:36:34.836
something along the lines
of equal opportunity?

00:36:34.836 --> 00:36:36.490
Are we struggling along that?

00:36:36.490 --> 00:36:41.914
How do we feel when there
are school districts

00:36:41.914 --> 00:36:48.349
that don't have anywhere
near adequate resources?

00:36:48.349 --> 00:36:53.140
It goes to nutrition, there is
nothing approaching equal education.

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:56.300
I broke the economy up into two parts so
that I could see it,

00:36:56.300 --> 00:36:59.160
because it gets lost in the averages.

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:03.380
So the top 40% and the bottom
60% because I wanted to see what

00:37:03.380 --> 00:37:07.910
the majority of people,
bottom 60%, is like.

00:37:07.910 --> 00:37:13.150
And certain things,
like the average in the top 40%,

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:18.570
they will spend five times as much
on their children's education

00:37:18.570 --> 00:37:24.780
than in the bottom 60%, and school
districts are starved for resources.

00:37:25.870 --> 00:37:28.800
And there's a whole different life,
I can rattle it off.

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:32.700
If you want to go see it,
I'd really love you to go see it.

00:37:32.700 --> 00:37:33.460
It's a study.

00:37:34.760 --> 00:37:39.116
That's on LinkedIn and
it shows the outcomes.

00:37:39.116 --> 00:37:44.031
So I guess when I'm looking at
that I would say that's not I

00:37:44.031 --> 00:37:48.545
think a fair system and
beyond that it's a system that

00:37:48.545 --> 00:37:53.840
threatens to destroy us if we
don't find a way to reform it.

00:37:53.840 --> 00:37:55.430
Now I'm a capitalist,

00:37:55.430 --> 00:38:00.840
I do believe in the idea of working
hard and earning and then saving.

00:38:00.840 --> 00:38:07.120
And what capitalism is, is you buy an
instrument of capitalism, you buy stocks.

00:38:07.120 --> 00:38:08.210
That's capitalism.

00:38:08.210 --> 00:38:10.070
You own a piece of that business.

00:38:10.070 --> 00:38:12.400
And I believe that that's fantastic.

00:38:12.400 --> 00:38:18.310
But like anything, everything has gotta
be improved with time, with reformation.

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:21.690
So are we producing
the outcomes that we want?

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:25.790
So when you read that, you'll see if
we're producing the outcomes we want,

00:38:25.790 --> 00:38:26.970
I don't think so.

00:38:26.970 --> 00:38:30.030
So I'm saying I think
we have to modify that.

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:35.700
Now, how we do that is going to,
most importantly,

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:41.140
it has to be a good engineering
exercise that I think has to be first.

00:38:41.140 --> 00:38:44.050
It's gotta be done in a bipartisan way.

00:38:44.050 --> 00:38:46.420
Because I think this
division is a problems and

00:38:46.420 --> 00:38:48.750
I really believe in
thoughtful disagreement.

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:54.050
So if you bring together people who
will living in these communities and

00:38:54.050 --> 00:38:59.180
then you have metrics, I think it
would be great if the leadership,

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:00.840
it could be leadership
of the United States,

00:39:00.840 --> 00:39:06.220
it could be the leadership of the state,
says that this is a critical

00:39:06.220 --> 00:39:11.010
issue that we have to deal with that they
put metrics on it so they own in it.

00:39:11.010 --> 00:39:16.850
In other words, numbers and KPIs and
they say, here's the condition.

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:19.790
Is that living standard the same or
is it rising?

00:39:19.790 --> 00:39:22.340
What are we doing in terms of education?

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:27.280
And with those measures, then then work
together in terms of doing sensible

00:39:27.280 --> 00:39:31.680
engineering I have my own
views of what could be done.

00:39:31.680 --> 00:39:35.510
But how we get into that,
that's a whole conversation.

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:39.010
I'm not so sure my views should matter.

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:40.470
This is just one of other views.

00:39:40.470 --> 00:39:45.720
But if we don't do it together and
we don't treat it as a really important

00:39:45.720 --> 00:39:49.380
critical issue, I think the most
critical issue of our time right now,

00:39:49.380 --> 00:39:52.840
I think we're going to be in trouble.

00:39:52.840 --> 00:40:00.021
So yes, of course, But there needs
to be redistribution of opportunity.

00:40:00.021 --> 00:40:05.186
It's not just redistribution of money,
but get the school books,

00:40:05.186 --> 00:40:10.993
get the nutrition, get those basic things,
I think that's fundamental.

00:40:10.993 --> 00:40:13.926
&gt;&gt; Well, Ray,
we're at least out of time for now.

00:40:13.926 --> 00:40:19.494
I want to make sure our audience here have
the chance to ask their questions of you.

00:40:19.494 --> 00:40:22.126
So anyone out there who has a question,
please raise your hand.

00:40:22.126 --> 00:40:24.035
We'll find you with the microphone.

00:40:24.035 --> 00:40:26.415
And then if you're selected,
please stand up and

00:40:26.415 --> 00:40:28.859
introduce yourself before
you ask your question.

00:40:36.873 --> 00:40:40.075
&gt;&gt; Hello, my name is Max, I'm MBA 1.

00:40:40.075 --> 00:40:45.160
I'm from Russia, so sorry, I don't know
the local political landscape that well.

00:40:45.160 --> 00:40:49.070
But my question is having such
a great reputation, knowledge, and

00:40:49.070 --> 00:40:52.980
leadership experience in this country,
why you are not running for

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:55.120
the president of the United States?

00:40:55.120 --> 00:41:00.108
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Because I don't think me and

00:41:00.108 --> 00:41:03.356
the political system would get along.

00:41:03.356 --> 00:41:08.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think it's a very difficult system.

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:09.950
I don't think it would be effective.

00:41:09.950 --> 00:41:14.541
I think the political system,
even the way it's structured,

00:41:14.541 --> 00:41:18.189
where the two parties and
the way they go about it.

00:41:18.189 --> 00:41:23.967
And I think it's a challenge
that's almost impossible to win.

00:41:23.967 --> 00:41:31.071
And anyway, I'm able, at this moment
in time, this short moment in time,

00:41:31.071 --> 00:41:36.510
to pass along some thoughts
that others in that position,

00:41:36.510 --> 00:41:40.178
or any position, could take or leave.

00:41:45.752 --> 00:41:46.740
&gt;&gt; Thanks for being here.

00:41:46.740 --> 00:41:47.630
I'm Ida Girma.

00:41:47.630 --> 00:41:49.170
I'm a first year MBA student.

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:50.210
I have two questions.

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:52.390
If you want to be fair,
you could just pick one to answer.

00:41:53.670 --> 00:41:58.150
One is, we understand that you're really
quite quantitative and systematic,

00:41:58.150 --> 00:42:02.240
about the way you think about decisions
for investments, for your business, and

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:03.200
personal life as well.

00:42:03.200 --> 00:42:07.957
I'm wondering what you think about this
notion of intuition, do you believe in it,

00:42:07.957 --> 00:42:08.761
do you use it?

00:42:08.761 --> 00:42:10.773
And my second question is,

00:42:10.773 --> 00:42:15.770
I'm quite inspired by how you talk
about equality of opportunity.

00:42:15.770 --> 00:42:17.160
I think it's fantastic.

00:42:17.160 --> 00:42:18.920
I suspect that you have peers and

00:42:18.920 --> 00:42:21.471
colleagues who may not
think the same things.

00:42:21.471 --> 00:42:25.068
And how do you have conversations with
them about this thing that you're so

00:42:25.068 --> 00:42:26.050
passionate about?

00:42:31.115 --> 00:42:34.577
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so
what was the first question again?

00:42:34.577 --> 00:42:38.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Intuition, yeah,

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:44.970
I think decision making is subliminal and
logical.

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:49.590
And fabulous ideas come from
subliminal decision making.

00:42:49.590 --> 00:42:52.278
But you don't know if they're great or
not,

00:42:52.278 --> 00:42:55.540
that you have to reconcile
them with the logic.

00:42:55.540 --> 00:43:01.290
So yes, intuition can be very,
very valuable.

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:03.530
We feel it, you see it, it's subliminal.

00:43:03.530 --> 00:43:09.326
The ability to collect thoughts,
connect dots, and see things in that way.

00:43:09.326 --> 00:43:16.160
Imagination, all of that, some of
the best ideas come in a subliminal way.

00:43:16.160 --> 00:43:21.140
Like if you're taking a hot shower, and
these ideas come to you, it's not like

00:43:21.140 --> 00:43:25.970
you're sitting there working hard on those
ideas, they come up, they can be fabulous.

00:43:25.970 --> 00:43:29.100
I think the key is to
reconcile them with your logic

00:43:29.100 --> 00:43:30.780
because they could be misleading, too.

00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:38.088
So yes, I would say they're at least
as valuable as the logical idea.

00:43:38.088 --> 00:43:44.132
And the second question was about what?

00:43:44.132 --> 00:43:48.072
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How do you have those conversations

00:43:48.072 --> 00:43:49.322
with your peers?

00:43:49.322 --> 00:43:52.990
&gt;&gt; Just the art of
thoughtful disagreement.

00:43:52.990 --> 00:43:54.080
I love disagreement.

00:43:54.080 --> 00:43:57.773
I just have to say what I believe,
and others say what they believe, and

00:43:57.773 --> 00:43:59.756
then we have a conversation about it.

00:43:59.756 --> 00:44:03.350
And I love it to do that openly.

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:07.200
So if you were to sit here and have a
conversation, I would welcome anybody who

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:10.400
has a different point of view,
have a thoughtful conversation.

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:11.250
That's easy.

00:44:12.430 --> 00:44:16.510
I don't see any reason why there shouldn't
be good, thoughtful disagreement.

00:44:19.587 --> 00:44:23.144
&gt;&gt; Hi, Ray,
I'm also a first year MBA student.

00:44:23.144 --> 00:44:26.070
I know you remember the giving pledge.

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:30.655
So I was wondering what principles
you apply to your giving in order to

00:44:30.655 --> 00:44:35.557
maximize your social return on investment
in the same way you did with your

00:44:35.557 --> 00:44:36.997
financial returns.

00:44:36.997 --> 00:44:40.188
&gt;&gt; The saying that it's tougher to give
away money than it is to make it is

00:44:40.188 --> 00:44:40.968
actually true.

00:44:40.968 --> 00:44:43.407
Because when you're pursuing a profit,

00:44:43.407 --> 00:44:47.308
you will know whether you're succeeding or
failing.

00:44:47.308 --> 00:44:52.001
When you're giving away money,
it's very difficult to have those kinds of

00:44:52.001 --> 00:44:56.274
metrics and weigh one thing or
another, it's a lot more confusing.

00:44:56.274 --> 00:45:00.619
But I tend to take the same approach
that I would in my investing.

00:45:00.619 --> 00:45:05.062
Meaning, I try to think in terms
of return on investment, but

00:45:05.062 --> 00:45:09.432
I think in terms of double bottom
line return on investment.

00:45:09.432 --> 00:45:12.452
In other words, what is the social impact?

00:45:12.452 --> 00:45:18.164
It has to stretch your mind,
because how do you relate what is

00:45:18.164 --> 00:45:24.001
the value of saving a life,
what is the value of an education?

00:45:24.001 --> 00:45:27.770
It doesn't have a common denominator
like return on investment has a number,

00:45:27.770 --> 00:45:31.360
it's money, and it's how that is,
so that becomes a challenge.

00:45:31.360 --> 00:45:34.730
For me, it's very important,
though, to try to do that.

00:45:34.730 --> 00:45:40.030
So we'll establish key performance
indicators and do that.

00:45:40.030 --> 00:45:44.113
But also relating to me is the passion.

00:45:44.113 --> 00:45:48.140
I've decided,
how am I going to take that approach?

00:45:48.140 --> 00:45:53.800
And that approach,
I make it a family activity.

00:45:53.800 --> 00:45:56.841
My family members,
different in varying degrees,

00:45:56.841 --> 00:46:00.360
are interested in their
own philanthropic efforts.

00:46:00.360 --> 00:46:03.630
And each one of them has
their own particular passion.

00:46:03.630 --> 00:46:04.951
I think that's great.

00:46:04.951 --> 00:46:09.808
So I would say that when you get past the
certain point, what you think about is,

00:46:09.808 --> 00:46:12.120
what do you really want to do?

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:16.420
You don't think about profit as much as
you think about, what do you want to do?

00:46:16.420 --> 00:46:20.470
And I think maybe as you get older or
you feel more connected, and

00:46:20.470 --> 00:46:23.470
you also realize
the blessings that I've had,

00:46:23.470 --> 00:46:27.228
because I didn't have anything,
and then I've evolved.

00:46:27.228 --> 00:46:30.652
And you see what other people are is
that marginal benefit of spending

00:46:30.652 --> 00:46:35.020
money on yourself and to being able to
help other people makes a big difference.

00:46:35.020 --> 00:46:39.270
So those things, return on investment,
how do I make it sustainable?

00:46:39.270 --> 00:46:40.987
How do I measure?

00:46:40.987 --> 00:46:44.700
How do I judge how good
we are doing as a family?

00:46:44.700 --> 00:46:46.640
How do I establish principles?

00:46:46.640 --> 00:46:50.340
We've got a whole bunch of principles
that people have to abide by

00:46:50.340 --> 00:46:51.430
in order to do it well.

00:46:51.430 --> 00:46:53.550
So it's similar, but

00:46:53.550 --> 00:46:58.700
it's more difficult because of the fact
that you can't account for it very well.

00:46:58.700 --> 00:47:02.948
So if you were to think,
what should you give money to?

00:47:02.948 --> 00:47:09.030
Are you going to be able to measure
whether education here or life?

00:47:09.030 --> 00:47:13.110
Those are difficult questions to answer,
but you work yourself through it.

00:47:13.110 --> 00:47:15.910
I joined the Giving Pledge
mostly because I learn a lot.

00:47:17.550 --> 00:47:21.655
The people in there are in somewhat
similar circumstances, and

00:47:21.655 --> 00:47:25.159
they brainstorm well,and
it's a learning process.

00:47:28.042 --> 00:47:30.540
&gt;&gt; All right, Reuben Arnold, MBA 2.

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:35.374
So I know you've tried to transition out
of the company and transitioned controlled

00:47:35.374 --> 00:47:39.813
the company several times last year as
you're moving into this third phase.

00:47:39.813 --> 00:47:42.763
I was wondering if you could speak a
little bit to the dynamics that might have

00:47:42.763 --> 00:47:44.110
made that transition difficult.

00:47:45.280 --> 00:47:49.507
&gt;&gt; Well, I fortunately had this principle.

00:47:49.507 --> 00:47:50.564
Here's the background.

00:47:50.564 --> 00:47:56.410
Something like nine years ago,
I decided that I wanted to transition

00:47:56.410 --> 00:48:00.770
It was important thing and I didn't
know how long it was going to take me.

00:48:00.770 --> 00:48:05.180
I thought it would take me about two
years, because I was working with

00:48:05.180 --> 00:48:08.470
the people for a number of years and
I thought it was going to be great.

00:48:08.470 --> 00:48:14.037
But then on the other hand, I learned
never to believe what I think is a theory.

00:48:14.037 --> 00:48:17.049
If I haven't done something
before successfully

00:48:17.049 --> 00:48:19.720
then I can't assume that
I know how to do it.

00:48:19.720 --> 00:48:24.550
So I said then,
it maybe could take up to ten years.

00:48:24.550 --> 00:48:28.480
I think it took me about eight and
a half years, in order to do that well.

00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:33.720
So you go down a journey,
I didn't know anything about governance.

00:48:33.720 --> 00:48:38.600
I had just run the company and
with this idea of meritocracy that I put

00:48:38.600 --> 00:48:42.630
myself in a position that I would
report to this management committee.

00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:43.680
They can hire, fire me.

00:48:43.680 --> 00:48:45.090
They could operate that way.

00:48:45.090 --> 00:48:49.010
So that I had myself held accountable
to this idea of meritocracy.

00:48:49.010 --> 00:48:52.500
And I thought this thing was going to
work, I made the transition.

00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:56.890
And then I, with a difficulty,
realized that it wouldn't work.

00:48:56.890 --> 00:48:59.560
And we all agreed that it wasn't working.

00:48:59.560 --> 00:49:03.860
And then we set up a governance system,
the proper board.

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:08.587
And I realized, I learned a lot about
governance, and I learned about how

00:49:08.587 --> 00:49:13.391
to pass those responsibilities along
to others and then we work that out and

00:49:13.391 --> 00:49:17.683
it's been about a year and
a half since I transitioned out of that.

00:49:17.683 --> 00:49:22.803
So I think the lesson is, I have a saying,
if you haven't done something three times

00:49:22.803 --> 00:49:27.990
before successfully, don't assume you can
do it and that was really helpful to me.

00:49:27.990 --> 00:49:32.000
So through that trial and
error was part of the learning process.

00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:35.430
So I think that's the saying,
I guess that would be the principle.

00:49:35.430 --> 00:49:38.321
If you haven't done something
successfully three times before,

00:49:38.321 --> 00:49:39.879
don't assume you know how to do it.

00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:47.602
&gt;&gt; Hi, Alyssa, I'm an MBA 1.

00:49:47.602 --> 00:49:50.688
And when people talk about
economic opportunity,

00:49:50.688 --> 00:49:55.280
a lot of the times, we talk about
children of the poor becoming rich.

00:49:55.280 --> 00:49:59.320
But we rarely talk about children of
the rich making room for other people.

00:49:59.320 --> 00:50:03.590
And so I was wondering if your
emphasis on equal opportunity

00:50:03.590 --> 00:50:05.170
affected the way that you parent.

00:50:06.280 --> 00:50:10.770
&gt;&gt; Well, yes, see, but I think I had
an experience because, and I think my kids

00:50:10.770 --> 00:50:16.250
were lucky because I didn't have,
I had the most important things.

00:50:16.250 --> 00:50:18.190
I should say I didn't have anything.

00:50:18.190 --> 00:50:21.930
I had parents who loved me.

00:50:21.930 --> 00:50:26.070
I had adequate nutrition,
I was well taken care of,

00:50:26.070 --> 00:50:31.449
I went to a public high school but
I didn't have much more than that.

00:50:31.449 --> 00:50:36.953
And then, for most of my life,
my kids didn't have much more than that,

00:50:36.953 --> 00:50:41.920
so they know the difference,
they know what the marginal utility

00:50:41.920 --> 00:50:46.470
of having a lot is relative to that,
not very much.

00:50:46.470 --> 00:50:49.420
And then you start to
create that sense of value.

00:50:49.420 --> 00:50:53.570
So my own experience
affected my reaction to it.

00:50:53.570 --> 00:50:58.110
I don't know what it would
be like to be born rich and

00:50:58.110 --> 00:51:01.320
then find out what that's like,
I don't know what that would be like.

00:51:01.320 --> 00:51:05.330
It must be much more
difficult in terms of that.

00:51:05.330 --> 00:51:09.200
So my experience, naturally,
told me the differences.

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:13.130
And I think that it's important to
understand there's been a number of

00:51:13.130 --> 00:51:15.190
studies on happiness around the world.

00:51:16.640 --> 00:51:20.840
Probably Stanford got their professors
who are the ones study happiness.

00:51:20.840 --> 00:51:24.000
And the one thing is that
they found that there's

00:51:24.000 --> 00:51:28.550
no correlation past the basic
level of money and happiness.

00:51:28.550 --> 00:51:33.418
But the one thing that is,
the most important thing that seems to

00:51:33.418 --> 00:51:37.488
have crossed communities
is the sense of community.

00:51:37.488 --> 00:51:40.350
Your relationships,
your personal relationships.

00:51:40.350 --> 00:51:44.790
And so I think that that's
affected my approach but

00:51:44.790 --> 00:51:48.820
I wouldn't know how to do it if I
didn't experience it, I don't think.

00:51:50.420 --> 00:51:52.141
&gt;&gt; Maybe time for one more question?

00:52:05.019 --> 00:52:05.870
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm David.

00:52:05.870 --> 00:52:07.490
And I'm a third year undergrad.

00:52:07.490 --> 00:52:08.910
In your interviews and your book,

00:52:08.910 --> 00:52:11.980
you talked about some great leaders
like Steve Jobs and Lee Kuan Yew.

00:52:11.980 --> 00:52:14.870
Could you talk about some of your
favorite stories of great character

00:52:14.870 --> 00:52:16.090
from people like that?

00:52:16.090 --> 00:52:17.250
&gt;&gt; Great character?

00:52:17.250 --> 00:52:18.950
&gt;&gt; Stories of great character.

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:20.120
&gt;&gt; Stories of great character.

00:52:21.840 --> 00:52:28.640
Well, different qualities
in those two people.

00:52:28.640 --> 00:52:33.274
But, I think Lee Kuan Yew

00:52:33.274 --> 00:52:38.350
was a person who was wise and

00:52:38.350 --> 00:52:44.551
balanced and self sacrificing.

00:52:44.551 --> 00:52:51.293
A great leader who had the vision and
the common sense and

00:52:51.293 --> 00:52:56.027
the wisdom to also suffer the slings and

00:52:56.027 --> 00:53:03.777
arrows of his position to make
Singapore a very special place and

00:53:03.777 --> 00:53:09.060
I had the opportunity
to know him a little.

00:53:09.060 --> 00:53:10.790
And he was remarkable people.

00:53:10.790 --> 00:53:16.840
But I think that there were,
if you read Joseph Campbell's book,

00:53:16.840 --> 00:53:21.926
Hero of a Thousand Faces, you can
almost say what are the archetypical

00:53:21.926 --> 00:53:25.680
characteristics of great leaders.

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:31.220
And there's this evolution
that's described in that book,

00:53:31.220 --> 00:53:35.780
in which people with the sense
of adventure and caring,

00:53:35.780 --> 00:53:41.730
they go out on their adventure,
and then they are on a mission.

00:53:41.730 --> 00:53:44.997
And they succeed and they fail.

00:53:44.997 --> 00:53:46.800
And they do it with other people.

00:53:46.800 --> 00:53:52.090
And that the mission and the other people

00:53:52.090 --> 00:53:57.440
eventually becomes more important
to them than themselves.

00:53:57.440 --> 00:54:02.640
And then they start to rise in
terms of seeing things different.

00:54:02.640 --> 00:54:05.520
Which I think is the essence
of what spirituality is.

00:54:05.520 --> 00:54:08.180
Spirituality, I don't mean religion.

00:54:08.180 --> 00:54:12.530
I mean, the sense of connectedness
to others in a whole.

00:54:12.530 --> 00:54:17.960
And when that becomes more
important to them then

00:54:17.960 --> 00:54:22.170
themselves, they become,
that's what constitutes being a hero.

00:54:22.170 --> 00:54:26.700
And so people like Lee Kuan Yew and
such people had those

00:54:26.700 --> 00:54:31.350
particular qualities and
made, and did what they did.

00:54:31.350 --> 00:54:34.320
So there are many, many, many cases.

00:54:34.320 --> 00:54:41.246
I mean I'm saying this man over here,
HR McMasters, is for me a hero for me.

00:54:41.246 --> 00:54:43.577
And so I see it all around me and

00:54:43.577 --> 00:54:47.880
I think it is those qualities
that make a person a hero.

00:54:49.260 --> 00:54:50.990
&gt;&gt; Well, Ray,
we've got a few minutes left.

00:54:50.990 --> 00:54:54.410
You talked about connectedness in
touchy feely language I might say.

00:54:54.410 --> 00:54:55.975
I feel more connected to you now.

00:54:55.975 --> 00:54:58.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And you've been very generous.

00:54:58.930 --> 00:55:03.060
You've shared your principles with us and
so as a token of our gratitude we thought

00:55:03.060 --> 00:55:06.910
we might share some of our
principles with you so this is.

00:55:06.910 --> 00:55:09.750
I thought it would be a thin book,
it's actually near as thick as yours.

00:55:09.750 --> 00:55:12.900
So this is some of RGSP principles from

00:55:12.900 --> 00:55:16.268
a lot of students here
in the audience today.

00:55:16.268 --> 00:55:17.256
&gt;&gt; Cool, yeah.

00:55:17.256 --> 00:55:19.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is for real.

00:55:19.360 --> 00:55:20.360
&gt;&gt; This is real.

00:55:20.360 --> 00:55:21.734
&gt;&gt; This is great.

00:55:21.734 --> 00:55:23.473
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:55:23.473 --> 00:55:24.682
These are your principles.

00:55:24.682 --> 00:55:25.889
&gt;&gt; These are our principles.

00:55:25.889 --> 00:55:26.817
&gt;&gt; On this app.

00:55:26.817 --> 00:55:28.860
I do want to take-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:55:28.860 --> 00:55:30.550
&gt;&gt; One of the good things on the app,

00:55:30.550 --> 00:55:32.650
there's part of the app that is a coach.

00:55:32.650 --> 00:55:38.090
And one of the things on it is
the coach allows you to describe,

00:55:38.090 --> 00:55:39.600
I'm in this situation.

00:55:39.600 --> 00:55:43.580
What principle is best for that situation?

00:55:43.580 --> 00:55:45.620
Because you just don't
want a pile of principles.

00:55:45.620 --> 00:55:48.530
You want to go to find the ones
that are most appropriate.

00:55:48.530 --> 00:55:51.020
And one of the most
important things about it

00:55:51.020 --> 00:55:53.070
is it allows you to put
your principles in.

00:55:54.460 --> 00:55:59.859
And so you could take your principles and
drop it into that coach.

00:55:59.859 --> 00:56:03.350
I'm going to be really interested,
I think that's the most important thing.

00:56:03.350 --> 00:56:08.920
I think we're in an era where
we're confused about principles.

00:56:08.920 --> 00:56:12.927
I don't think we spend enough
time thinking about principles,

00:56:12.927 --> 00:56:14.790
we think about doing so much.

00:56:14.790 --> 00:56:18.718
And maybe as religions have become
less important and other things,

00:56:18.718 --> 00:56:20.650
we don't think about that.

00:56:20.650 --> 00:56:23.190
So I think it's so
valuable that you've done it, and

00:56:23.190 --> 00:56:25.230
I think it's a real treat, a great gift.

00:56:25.230 --> 00:56:26.060
Thank you very much.

00:56:26.060 --> 00:56:27.010
I really appreciate it.

00:56:27.010 --> 00:56:27.745
&gt;&gt; And you're very welcome.

00:56:27.745 --> 00:56:30.515
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Wait, wait, wait, one sec, one sec.

00:56:30.515 --> 00:56:36.009
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; We're actually not quite done yet.

00:56:36.009 --> 00:56:37.860
You're not right off the hook.

00:56:39.050 --> 00:56:40.710
There's one more thing.

00:56:40.710 --> 00:56:43.120
In the interest of radical truth and

00:56:43.120 --> 00:56:48.350
radical transparency, we need to clear
a few rumors up about you, okay?

00:56:48.350 --> 00:56:51.110
So I'm going to give you
a series of statements.

00:56:51.110 --> 00:56:54.150
And I want you to tell us if
these are true or false, okay?

00:56:56.030 --> 00:57:00.930
You played a pivotal role in
the creation of the Chicken McNugget.

00:57:00.930 --> 00:57:01.746
&gt;&gt; I thought so.

00:57:01.746 --> 00:57:05.933
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Your devotion to meat

00:57:05.933 --> 00:57:10.730
commodities led you to name
your first son after a cow.

00:57:10.730 --> 00:57:11.294
&gt;&gt; True.

00:57:11.294 --> 00:57:14.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You caddied,

00:57:14.270 --> 00:57:19.620
as a child, for Richard Nixon and
the former king of England.

00:57:19.620 --> 00:57:20.850
&gt;&gt; True.

00:57:20.850 --> 00:57:21.650
&gt;&gt; Who's the better golfer?

00:57:23.130 --> 00:57:24.249
&gt;&gt; Richard Nixon, actually.

00:57:25.619 --> 00:57:27.375
&gt;&gt; And he wasn't a very good golfer.

00:57:27.375 --> 00:57:31.618
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Could imagine he's in the water a lot.

00:57:31.618 --> 00:57:35.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You were inspired by the Beatles

00:57:35.500 --> 00:57:39.610
to take up meditation, and
you still practice 50 years later.

00:57:39.610 --> 00:57:42.630
&gt;&gt; True, and one of the most important
things I think, it's helped me a lot.

00:57:43.840 --> 00:57:47.850
&gt;&gt; You've committed to giving away over
half your wealth in your lifetime.

00:57:47.850 --> 00:57:48.350
&gt;&gt; Yeah, true.

00:57:50.240 --> 00:57:54.021
&gt;&gt; You exclusively fly JetBlue
out of loyalty to Joel.

00:57:54.021 --> 00:58:00.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE]

00:58:00.850 --> 00:58:03.159
&gt;&gt; He was chewing me out about that.

00:58:03.159 --> 00:58:06.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So, that's a no,
not yet, more roots.

00:58:07.790 --> 00:58:13.055
You've worked for 44 years at Bridgewater
but have never once got promoted.

00:58:13.055 --> 00:58:17.999
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I got demoted a few times.

00:58:17.999 --> 00:58:22.545
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] You secretly wish
that you went to Stanford?

00:58:22.545 --> 00:58:25.873
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No comment.

00:58:25.873 --> 00:58:28.726
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Ladies and gentlemen,

00:58:28.726 --> 00:58:29.777
Ray Dalio-
&gt;&gt; Too political.

00:58:29.777 --> 00:58:30.748
&gt;&gt; Too political, he's spoken.

00:58:30.748 --> 00:58:37.942
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:58:37.942 --> 00:58:39.465
